1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
Never has a car meant so much to so few.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:09,000
It's the model that everybody wants, but only a select few are bold enough to own.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Topless. Unobstructed.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:17,000
The convertible turns the act of driving into an experience of man, machine, and the elements.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Now, convertibles on modern levels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,540
Talk with any convertible owner and they'll tell you the same thing.

7
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:46,940
Driving a convertible is a one-of-a-kind experience.

8
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:49,860
It can always make you happy.

9
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:52,200
You cannot be sad when you drive a convertible.

10
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:53,920
You just can't.

11
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:55,420
Let's put it this way.

12
00:00:55,421 --> 00:00:57,520
I wouldn't consider anything but a convertible.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,160
It's just an amazing feeling.

14
00:01:04,161 --> 00:01:10,240
Lance Zuckerbrunn drives a Ferrari F355 convertible.

15
00:01:10,241 --> 00:01:17,960
Only a few hundred are built each year and command a price of more than $120,000.

16
00:01:17,961 --> 00:01:20,000
With a convertible, there's no top.

17
00:01:20,001 --> 00:01:26,020
You've got the sky, you've got the road, you've got the wind, you've got the sounds

18
00:01:26,021 --> 00:01:29,000
of the road, the tires hitting the pavement.

19
00:01:29,001 --> 00:01:33,060
I mean, just your part of nature and machinery all at one time.

20
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:39,960
With a 3.5-liter dual-overhead cam V8 engine,

21
00:01:40,780 --> 00:01:47,680
its 375 horses can produce speeds in excess of 170 miles per hour.

22
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:49,840
You want me to punch it?

23
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:52,180
All right, here we go.

24
00:01:52,181 --> 00:01:59,320
That was 65 miles an hour.

25
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:02,320
What was that, about three seconds?

26
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:08,500
Let's call it four and a half, just because I did get a little excited myself.

27
00:02:10,820 --> 00:02:14,740
But there's no sense having a car this exciting if it's not a convertible.

28
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:20,860
The coolest thing about this car, I tell you, is just simply, besides the engine, being

29
00:02:20,861 --> 00:02:24,090
a convertible is just being able to do that.

30
00:02:24,091 --> 00:02:27,100
I mean, where else, how else can you just

31
00:02:27,101 --> 00:02:34,860
put your hands up and go, I'm free, baby. Freedom. That's the defining experience of

32
00:02:34,861 --> 00:02:40,229
the convertible. Cruising down the road with nothing but sky overhead.

33
00:02:40,230 --> 00:02:41,840
But there's something

34
00:02:41,841 --> 00:02:42,541
more.

35
00:02:42,542 --> 00:02:50,700
No matter the year, no matter the make, only the convertible gives you the option

36
00:02:50,701 --> 00:02:53,730
of open-topped or closed car driving.

37
00:02:53,731 --> 00:02:58,480
A convertible is essentially a closed car that offers open

38
00:02:58,481 --> 00:03:03,500
air motoring as an option. When the top is up, it's fully enclosed. Normally, it has

39
00:03:03,501 --> 00:03:04,474
wind-up windows.

40
00:03:04,475 --> 00:03:08,920
The top more than likely would be padded, so you have a nice, comfortable

41
00:03:08,921 --> 00:03:09,621
closed car.

42
00:03:09,622 --> 00:03:13,200
And when you want it to be open, you just put the top down, roll down the windows,

43
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:16,380
and all of a sudden you've got a car that's open to the elements.

44
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:22,340
But not every open-top car is a convertible.

45
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:31,840
Roadsters, for example, are two-seat open-top cars, but they have only a meager top and no windows.

46
00:03:31,841 --> 00:03:38,740
Touring cars, another topless option in the early days of the automobile, have unlined

47
00:03:38,741 --> 00:03:43,720
tops and only side curtains for protection.

48
00:03:43,721 --> 00:03:49,960
Neither roadsters nor touring cars are convertibles, since neither offers the choice of being fully

49
00:03:49,961 --> 00:03:52,840
protected from the elements.

50
00:03:52,841 --> 00:03:58,360
The first true convertibles were only offered around 1915.

51
00:03:58,361 --> 00:04:05,600
Before then, driving was a dirty, dusty, hazardous experience.

52
00:04:05,601 --> 00:04:11,140
Virtually all cars were open, though later touring cars came with a flimsy top that did

53
00:04:11,141 --> 00:04:15,600
little more than protect the passengers from the rain.

54
00:04:15,601 --> 00:04:20,620
As the demand for closed cars was growing, the manufacturers had a problem.

55
00:04:20,621 --> 00:04:26,000
The cabins of the early automobiles were made of wood, making a closed car too heavy for

56
00:04:26,001 --> 00:04:29,600
for the small four-cylinder engines being used.

57
00:04:29,601 --> 00:04:32,760
By the mid-teens, however, engines became efficient enough

58
00:04:32,761 --> 00:04:35,760
to generate upwards of 20 horsepower,

59
00:04:35,761 --> 00:04:39,160
and the closed cabin car came to market.

60
00:04:39,161 --> 00:04:43,040
Even Henry Ford finally bent to the demand

61
00:04:43,041 --> 00:04:45,440
for closed automobiles in 1915,

62
00:04:45,441 --> 00:04:49,000
building the first all-closed car called the Center Door Sedan,

63
00:04:49,001 --> 00:04:51,760
and then building a model that was a convertible,

64
00:04:51,761 --> 00:04:53,160
the first Ford convertible.

65
00:04:53,161 --> 00:04:54,520
1915 was called a couplet.

66
00:04:57,160 --> 00:05:01,160
The 1915 Ford couplet had a number of firsts.

67
00:05:02,020 --> 00:05:03,980
It was the first Ford to have electric lighting,

68
00:05:04,660 --> 00:05:06,360
the famous Ford four-cylinder engine,

69
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:10,560
and here was a transformable top.

70
00:05:11,960 --> 00:05:14,900
What made the couplet really so unique, though,

71
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:16,060
was not the top.

72
00:05:16,360 --> 00:05:19,500
That was a standard, manually folded canvas covering.

73
00:05:19,501 --> 00:05:24,500
What made the couplet a true convertible were its windows.

74
00:05:24,501 --> 00:05:26,500
There is a window mechanism.

75
00:05:26,501 --> 00:05:29,500
These fold up.

76
00:05:29,501 --> 00:05:34,500
And then the window pulls up, just like in the old horse-drawn era,

77
00:05:34,501 --> 00:05:37,500
and sat in place.

78
00:05:37,501 --> 00:05:43,500
And then when the door was closed, it was a fully enclosed vehicle.

79
00:05:43,501 --> 00:05:48,500
Soon, the idea of a convertible car swept through the marketplace.

80
00:05:48,501 --> 00:05:54,320
marketplace. Standard sedans were being re-engineered as convertibles, but their

81
00:05:54,321 --> 00:06:02,440
designs lacked charisma. That is, until the introduction of the 1927 LaSalle.

82
00:06:02,441 --> 00:06:09,680
Created by a Los Angeles body designer, Harley Earl, the $2,685 LaSalle was a

83
00:06:09,681 --> 00:06:15,980
dynamic breakthrough in car design. When its standard canvas top was down, the

84
00:06:15,981 --> 00:06:22,460
The LaSalle's new clamshell fenders and vertical front grille gave the car a low, compact look

85
00:06:22,461 --> 00:06:27,580
that no one had ever seen before on a production car.

86
00:06:27,581 --> 00:06:33,420
Other car companies immediately tried to emulate Harley Earl's new design by scouring his hometown

87
00:06:33,421 --> 00:06:36,740
of Los Angeles for the source of his ideas.

88
00:06:36,741 --> 00:06:39,320
But they were looking in the wrong place.

89
00:06:39,321 --> 00:06:45,120
It was not in California, but in Europe, and in particular, the cars of Hispano Suiza, where

90
00:06:45,121 --> 00:06:47,794
Harley Earl found his inspiration.

91
00:06:47,795 --> 00:06:52,040
Though the name literally means Spanish-Swiss, Hispano

92
00:06:52,041 --> 00:06:59,160
Suiza was actually a French car company, manufacturing luxury chassis during the early 20th century.

93
00:06:59,780 --> 00:07:02,557
This is a 1928 Hispano Suiza.

94
00:07:02,558 --> 00:07:07,920
This is called the model H6C, Hispano, six-cylinder, and

95
00:07:07,921 --> 00:07:12,680
their larger engine. And it was a takeoff from their wonderful aircraft engine. And

96
00:07:12,681 --> 00:07:16,020
The engine, though, was the most spectacular part of this automobile.

97
00:07:16,021 --> 00:07:22,120
It was a single overhead cam, six-cylinder engine, and absolutely fabulous looking.

98
00:07:22,880 --> 00:07:32,300
The 479 cubic inch engine could generate 130 horsepower and was capable of speeds of 110 miles per hour,

99
00:07:32,980 --> 00:07:34,760
thanks to its twin everything.

100
00:07:35,320 --> 00:07:40,760
Twin ignition system, twin starters, twin distributors, and twin water pumps.

101
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:46,160
All this sophistication came at an incredible price for the 1920s.

102
00:07:47,300 --> 00:07:50,240
The chassis alone, delivered here in the United States,

103
00:07:50,380 --> 00:07:52,400
if you wanted to order one, was $10,000.

104
00:07:52,980 --> 00:07:54,260
$10,000 for the chassis.

105
00:07:55,020 --> 00:07:58,360
That's at a time when the average income was $2,400,

106
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:02,900
and the standard Ford Model A cost $420.

107
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:09,740
And for cars like the H6C, as it was for most luxury cars of the day,

108
00:08:10,700 --> 00:08:13,300
One company would supply the chassis and engine.

109
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:15,660
The coachwork was done elsewhere.

110
00:08:16,660 --> 00:08:22,940
With a complete package price of $15,000, not many H6Cs were sold in America.

111
00:08:23,780 --> 00:08:28,540
In fact, estimates are less than 30, but their influence was enormous.

112
00:08:28,880 --> 00:08:38,760
The following years, car companies like Auburn, Cadillac, Packard, and Cord all had stylish convertibles on their showroom floors.

113
00:08:38,761 --> 00:08:44,760
While they were technically little different than hardtop coupes, there was no mistaking their appeal.

114
00:08:44,761 --> 00:08:50,760
By the late 20s, almost 20% of all cars built in the States were convertibles.

115
00:08:50,761 --> 00:08:55,760
But 1929 brought the crash. Black Friday.

116
00:08:55,761 --> 00:09:01,760
The worldwide economic depression clobbered the new car market, except for the convertible.

117
00:09:01,761 --> 00:09:06,760
for the convertible, the rich could still afford the stylish models, and car companies

118
00:09:06,761 --> 00:09:11,120
were creating even grander designs to catch their eye.

119
00:09:11,121 --> 00:09:17,280
When we return, the 1930s and the heyday of the convertible.

120
00:09:17,281 --> 00:09:20,420
Convertibles generally weigh more than their fixed roof counterpart.

121
00:09:20,421 --> 00:09:26,320
Their bodies have heavy reinforcing brackets needed to compensate for the absence of a

122
00:09:26,321 --> 00:09:27,320
roof.

123
00:09:27,321 --> 00:09:31,140
The 1930s was a remarkable time for the convertible.

124
00:09:31,141 --> 00:09:36,820
Though most of the world was still recovering from the stock market crash of 1929, the American

125
00:09:36,821 --> 00:09:43,140
elite's desire for the ultimate luxury automobile helped to find the decade's greatest convertibles.

126
00:09:43,141 --> 00:09:46,940
Well, I think they're works of art.

127
00:09:46,941 --> 00:09:52,640
You can look at these cars just standing still and they tell you a story and they're like

128
00:09:52,641 --> 00:09:55,300
a great painting.

129
00:09:55,301 --> 00:10:01,100
The hard edges of the 20s were being replaced by sleek, curvaceous lines and bold splashes

130
00:10:01,101 --> 00:10:02,100
of chrome.

131
00:10:02,101 --> 00:10:10,120
For the first time in their history, convertibles were sexy and high society was taking notice.

132
00:10:10,121 --> 00:10:18,800
Welcome to the world of the Kords, Lincolns, Packards and the Luminary Duesenbergs.

133
00:10:18,801 --> 00:10:22,695
Many people consider the ultimate driving machine was the fabulous J.

134
00:10:22,696 --> 00:10:23,880
Duesenbergs, produced

135
00:10:23,881 --> 00:10:28,280
by a small firm in Indianapolis, the Duesenberg brothers.

136
00:10:28,281 --> 00:10:30,980
The name often confused as being a German car,

137
00:10:30,981 --> 00:10:33,880
Duesenberg was an American car.

138
00:10:33,881 --> 00:10:37,480
By the early 1930s, Frederick and August Duesenberg

139
00:10:37,481 --> 00:10:40,680
had made a name for themselves as the premier engine builders

140
00:10:40,681 --> 00:10:42,840
of their day.

141
00:10:42,841 --> 00:10:46,620
The body styling, however, was left to others.

142
00:10:46,621 --> 00:10:49,820
When you bought a Duesenberg from the Indianapolis, Indiana

143
00:10:49,821 --> 00:10:53,040
plant, what you got was a chassis consisting

144
00:10:53,041 --> 00:10:59,040
of the frame rails, the engine, a drive train, the wheels, the steering wheel, even a dashboard.

145
00:10:59,620 --> 00:11:03,020
If you strapped an orange crate to the chassis, you could sit on it and go for a drive.

146
00:11:03,880 --> 00:11:06,060
That's what Duesenberg provided.

147
00:11:06,600 --> 00:11:09,480
So what had to be done is the car had to be sent to a coach builder.

148
00:11:11,340 --> 00:11:15,060
Dozens of custom coach builders, from California to Europe,

149
00:11:15,620 --> 00:11:20,780
took the same Duesenberg chassis and created some of the most stunning convertibles ever made.

150
00:11:23,040 --> 00:11:27,340
These designers bestowed the Duesenberg with long, elegant lines.

151
00:11:28,200 --> 00:11:35,220
The fact that its soft, flexible top could be hidden in a small space behind the cabin preserved the elegance of the design.

152
00:11:36,660 --> 00:11:40,920
There were, however, certain features deemed off-limits by the company.

153
00:11:41,700 --> 00:11:48,940
Ever since the 1929 J model, the Duesenberg's radiator, headlights, and hood ornament could not be altered.

154
00:11:49,260 --> 00:11:51,740
That's how you knew it was a Duesenberg.

155
00:11:51,741 --> 00:11:57,920
And to add to its snob appeal, there were no nameplates or logos splashed across the car.

156
00:11:58,740 --> 00:12:02,040
In fact, other than a tiny emblem on the front and wheel hubs,

157
00:12:02,820 --> 00:12:07,280
the name Duesenberg could only be found on the inside of the engine compartment,

158
00:12:07,740 --> 00:12:09,620
the heart and soul of the car.

159
00:12:10,780 --> 00:12:17,120
This particular model, called SJN, ours was built in 1936 on 144-inch wheelbase,

160
00:12:17,121 --> 00:12:23,400
a 17-inch wheel with a fabulous double overhead cam Duesenberg engine with supercharger.

161
00:12:24,160 --> 00:12:28,680
Many people like to think of these as being, say, 16 cylinders like a Cadillac,

162
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:31,320
but they made only straight eight engines at Duesenberg.

163
00:12:31,900 --> 00:12:37,300
104 miles an hour in second gear, so they said, and 129.8 in high gear.

164
00:12:40,260 --> 00:12:43,900
Exhaust cutouts added more than just a cocky display of chrome.

165
00:12:43,901 --> 00:12:50,300
They bypassed the muffler, reducing the back pressure to the engine and adding even more horsepower.

166
00:12:50,301 --> 00:12:55,100
It was just another innovation that made this car so distinctive.

167
00:12:55,101 --> 00:13:04,500
To compete with the Duesenbergs, elite car companies like Packard eliminated the cost for custom-designed bodywork by doing much of it themselves.

168
00:13:04,501 --> 00:13:11,100
Packard and Cadillac offered complete cars right from the factory.

169
00:13:11,101 --> 00:13:16,240
They were cataloged cars that were available that you could get in and just drive right out of the showroom.

170
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:22,700
As most cars were no longer custom built, their designs became somewhat more staid.

171
00:13:23,160 --> 00:13:27,700
But what Packard may have given up in styling, they made up for with their engine.

172
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:35,100
In the 1930s, Packard outdid the Duesenberg 8 when they offered a colossal V12 engine.

173
00:13:35,580 --> 00:13:39,700
It was the opening salvo in what was to become the Cylinder Wars.

174
00:13:41,100 --> 00:13:44,420
Packard had a 6 and an 8-cylinder and a 12-cylinder.

175
00:13:44,560 --> 00:13:45,460
This is a 12-cylinder.

176
00:13:45,560 --> 00:13:50,100
This would be the most expensive Packard that you could own, a big Packard 12.

177
00:13:50,780 --> 00:13:57,280
This is a 1937 factory-built V12, once owned by the movie star Betty Davis.

178
00:13:58,500 --> 00:14:01,440
This is about 185 horsepower, I think.

179
00:14:01,680 --> 00:14:04,920
This car would do an honest 100.

180
00:14:05,740 --> 00:14:09,760
You could cruise at 80, 85 on these cars in those days.

181
00:14:09,761 --> 00:14:13,580
Maybe the roads weren't very good, but the car was capable.

182
00:14:17,960 --> 00:14:24,660
These massive engines generated an enormous amount of heat and incorporated a brilliant new cooling system.

183
00:14:25,420 --> 00:14:28,520
The louvers of the front grille were thermostatically controlled.

184
00:14:29,800 --> 00:14:37,300
When the engine was first turned on, the louvers were closed, restricting the airflow to the engine, allowing it to warm up.

185
00:14:37,301 --> 00:14:41,540
As the engine's temperature rose, the louvers would open.

186
00:14:42,060 --> 00:14:45,220
Air would rush through the radiator, cooling the engine down.

187
00:14:45,900 --> 00:14:50,780
The constant adjustment of the louvers kept these powerful engines from overheating.

188
00:14:51,380 --> 00:14:54,620
The larger engines weren't only treasured for their power.

189
00:14:55,020 --> 00:14:59,700
The growing number of cylinders was creating smoother rides and less work for the drivers.

190
00:15:00,740 --> 00:15:03,020
People did not like to shift.

191
00:15:03,600 --> 00:15:07,180
If you had a car that was powerful and it had a lot of torque,

192
00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:09,620
it meant that you didn't have to shift as much.

193
00:15:10,560 --> 00:15:13,820
That's where multi-cylinder cars came in.

194
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:17,260
We're talking 12 cylinders and 16 cylinders.

195
00:15:17,940 --> 00:15:21,760
You're looking at probably the largest convertible automobile

196
00:15:21,761 --> 00:15:23,320
built here in the United States,

197
00:15:23,321 --> 00:15:28,180
a 1935 16-cylinder Cadillac Cabriolet.

198
00:15:33,020 --> 00:15:42,900
Four passengers only on a wheelbase of 157 inches and an overall length of over 19 and a half feet.

199
00:15:43,400 --> 00:15:47,620
A huge luxury automobile that Cadillac lost a fortune on.

200
00:15:48,760 --> 00:15:55,560
New peaks of luxurious motoring were scaled in 1930 when the first 16-cylinder car was offered to the public.

201
00:15:56,160 --> 00:15:59,480
Surely there was never before such a superb power plant as this.

202
00:15:59,481 --> 00:16:06,480
Cadillac's first V16 was actually introduced back on December 10, 1929.

203
00:16:06,481 --> 00:16:09,480
The timing could not have been worse.

204
00:16:09,481 --> 00:16:16,480
Just two months after the stock market crash, sales of the $7,000 cars were appalling.

205
00:16:16,481 --> 00:16:22,480
But Cadillac was convinced that the V16 was important to its image as a prestigious car manufacturer.

206
00:16:22,481 --> 00:16:28,480
So they continued the line, making it even grander in 1935.

207
00:16:28,481 --> 00:16:35,280
452 cubic inches and a really fine overhead valve system,

208
00:16:35,281 --> 00:16:38,500
which they came up with as being really very, very efficient,

209
00:16:39,020 --> 00:16:40,920
and which, of course, is being used today.

210
00:16:41,420 --> 00:16:45,500
Really a tremendous vehicle that you could actually start out in high gear.

211
00:16:46,340 --> 00:16:51,640
The impressive V16 engine was shared by both the convertibles and hardtops.

212
00:16:52,500 --> 00:16:57,580
Essentially a twin eight, the true beauty of the 32-valve engine was its performance.

213
00:16:58,420 --> 00:17:04,320
Firing 16 times for each two turns of the crankshaft, it was an exceptionally smooth-running engine.

214
00:17:06,220 --> 00:17:12,620
As impressive as it was, however, only 50 Cadillac V16s were produced in 1935.

215
00:17:13,800 --> 00:17:20,680
Apparently, the infatuation with style and power had priced the high-end American convertible into oblivion.

216
00:17:20,681 --> 00:17:31,800
The automotive superstars of the 1930s were certainly the ultra-luxury convertibles like the Duesenbergs,

217
00:17:31,801 --> 00:17:34,680
but they were not the only convertibles being produced.

218
00:17:35,860 --> 00:17:39,080
Oh, a new Ford. And what a lovely color.

219
00:17:40,060 --> 00:17:46,780
Traditional models like the Ford convertible sedan were selling for a fraction of the price of Packard and Kord convertibles.

220
00:17:47,460 --> 00:17:52,020
And though they might not have had the celebrity of cars like the V16 Cadillacs,

221
00:17:52,021 --> 00:17:58,100
by the end of the decade, one had something its rivals did not, an automatic top.

222
00:17:58,840 --> 00:18:03,180
Well, the very first power top offered on a production car was, believe it or not, Plymouth.

223
00:18:03,980 --> 00:18:07,180
Chrysler Corporation's bottom-of-the-line car, and that was 1939.

224
00:18:07,980 --> 00:18:11,020
This is a 1939 Plymouth Special Convertible.

225
00:18:11,820 --> 00:18:13,020
Actually, it's the Special Deluxe.

226
00:18:13,021 --> 00:18:17,020
And this is the first year of a power top.

227
00:18:19,820 --> 00:18:26,460
The lowly $900 Plymouth not only had the company's first column-mounted three-speed transmission,

228
00:18:27,040 --> 00:18:30,920
but its power top set a new standard for the convertible world.

229
00:18:32,120 --> 00:18:35,220
Here is the power top mechanism.

230
00:18:36,040 --> 00:18:36,920
Quite innovative.

231
00:18:36,921 --> 00:18:43,200
The top is a wonderful mechanism, and it is a very slow, but it is power operated.

232
00:18:43,840 --> 00:18:49,340
And the top is under here, and the motor is in between the rumble seat and the bottom of the boot.

233
00:18:50,860 --> 00:18:53,600
This is a rather unique top assembly.

234
00:18:54,080 --> 00:19:00,200
When you pull this center latch down, it pivots the outer two ones, and they hook up.

235
00:19:01,160 --> 00:19:05,160
You pull up to align it with air and push it right down.

236
00:19:05,161 --> 00:19:07,400
both arms go and lock it.

237
00:19:08,100 --> 00:19:08,920
Quite innovative.

238
00:19:10,960 --> 00:19:13,440
For the first time in automotive history,

239
00:19:13,800 --> 00:19:15,260
car owners could push a button

240
00:19:15,261 --> 00:19:16,960
and four pneumatic cylinders

241
00:19:16,961 --> 00:19:19,520
would magically raise and lower the top.

242
00:19:23,200 --> 00:19:24,980
It was a major achievement

243
00:19:24,981 --> 00:19:27,500
and the rest of the car world knew it.

244
00:19:28,560 --> 00:19:30,520
But just as automatic tops

245
00:19:30,521 --> 00:19:32,140
were coming off the assembly line,

246
00:19:32,141 --> 00:19:36,300
World War II brought civilian automotive production to a standstill.

247
00:19:37,180 --> 00:19:43,260
Duesenberg went out of business, and the Willys Jeep was on its way to becoming America's most popular ragtop.

248
00:19:44,180 --> 00:19:47,120
Soon you'll be able to give that jalopy a well-earned rest.

249
00:19:47,720 --> 00:19:54,160
With gas again plentiful, and with new cars and new tires on the way, America will be rolling with a pre-war flourish.

250
00:19:55,420 --> 00:20:00,680
Finally, in 1946, car manufacturers returned to domestic production.

251
00:20:00,681 --> 00:20:05,680
It would be two more years before the unique styling of the convertible would reemerge.

252
00:20:05,681 --> 00:20:11,680
Chrysler, after World War II, decided to come out with something very special.

253
00:20:11,681 --> 00:20:16,680
People were looking for something to kind of forget the war and kind of get into the new decade.

254
00:20:16,681 --> 00:20:20,680
And so Chrysler came out with this land yacht.

255
00:20:20,681 --> 00:20:25,680
In what was truly a bold design move, Chrysler virtually superimposed

256
00:20:25,681 --> 00:20:31,360
imposed the classic luxury wooden boats of the 30s onto their newest convertible designs.

257
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:37,920
They included an automatic electric top, the new semi-automatic fluid drive transmission,

258
00:20:40,480 --> 00:20:47,360
and the latest L-head 8 engine. But with more than 4,000 pounds to push,

259
00:20:47,361 --> 00:20:52,720
it didn't exactly burn rubber. Nevertheless, it was a big success for Chrysler.

260
00:20:52,721 --> 00:20:56,560
despite its high price of $2,725.

261
00:20:57,720 --> 00:21:02,920
Almost 10,000 town and country convertibles were sold in the four years it was offered.

262
00:21:05,080 --> 00:21:11,480
The bold look of the town and country was the first important design statement of the convertibles post-war era.

263
00:21:13,260 --> 00:21:20,700
Soon, the sheer size of the newest models, along with the tail fin, would become the dominant features of the 1950s cars.

264
00:21:20,701 --> 00:21:24,500
The 50s cars are just the epitome of the convertible.

265
00:21:24,501 --> 00:21:29,220
They were big and beautiful and lots of chrome.

266
00:21:29,221 --> 00:21:33,420
They're just like big old battleships.

267
00:21:33,421 --> 00:21:38,580
What made it all possible were the new, more efficient V8 engines.

268
00:21:38,581 --> 00:21:45,580
Whereas in the late 20s and into the 30s, you had V12 and V16 engines that powered luxury

269
00:21:45,581 --> 00:21:51,393
cars. In 1949, the overhead valve V8 really came into its own.

270
00:21:51,394 --> 00:21:54,300
It was a very efficient engine,

271
00:21:54,920 --> 00:21:59,840
and those could power Cadillacs and Oldsmobiles, the Rocket 88 Oldsmobile, for example,

272
00:22:00,140 --> 00:22:06,482
overhead valve V8, 1949. Those engines could power those cars to very high speeds.

273
00:22:06,483 --> 00:22:07,720
You could cruise

274
00:22:07,721 --> 00:22:11,800
all day at 70, 80 miles an hour, no problem.

275
00:22:13,460 --> 00:22:18,980
These new engines empowered designers to create grand behemoths of steel and chrome,

276
00:22:19,340 --> 00:22:21,640
both in their convertible and hardtop versions.

277
00:22:22,660 --> 00:22:27,760
Where convertibles came into their own was with the nifty sports cars of the 50s.

278
00:22:28,700 --> 00:22:33,120
Only a few designers were willing to buck the trend of the large convertible.

279
00:22:33,600 --> 00:22:36,120
One was General Motors' Harley Earl.

280
00:22:36,121 --> 00:22:43,060
In 1953, he introduced a new model, and the topless world was turned upside down.

281
00:22:43,400 --> 00:22:48,060
They call her Corvette, and she belongs to the highway.

282
00:22:48,980 --> 00:22:54,920
Codenamed Project Opal, Chevrolet introduced the Corvette in 1953.

283
00:22:55,820 --> 00:23:02,020
Small, sleek, lightweight, it was the antithesis of the 50s trend towards big,

284
00:23:02,021 --> 00:23:05,920
and reignited the desire for the open-topped roadster.

285
00:23:06,120 --> 00:23:12,420
But due to its automatic transmission and smaller engine, the 53 Corvette didn't sell well.

286
00:23:12,980 --> 00:23:20,020
Its styling, however, opened the door to a new world of convertibles, a door the Ford Thunderbird drove right through.

287
00:23:21,160 --> 00:23:26,940
Sold not as a sports car, but as a personal car, the T-Bird was a hit from the very beginning.

288
00:23:29,260 --> 00:23:33,500
The T-Bird outsold the Corvette nearly 23 to 1.

289
00:23:33,501 --> 00:23:38,940
Its comfortable ride and good looks made it the favorite of car enthusiasts and family

290
00:23:38,941 --> 00:23:40,940
men alike.

291
00:23:40,941 --> 00:23:46,580
The unique removable hardtop, which could be lifted off as a single piece, created an exciting

292
00:23:46,581 --> 00:23:50,940
new way to enjoy the open-topped experience.

293
00:23:50,941 --> 00:23:54,480
This is a 1957 Ford Thunderbird.

294
00:23:54,481 --> 00:23:58,380
It's the last year of the small birds, or the baby bird as they're called.

295
00:23:58,381 --> 00:24:06,980
It's 55, 56, and 57, and then they went to a four-passenger front and rear seat model.

296
00:24:07,680 --> 00:24:09,900
Dave Baker is a Thunderbird man.

297
00:24:09,901 --> 00:24:16,760
He owns the latest T-Bird convertible, the retro-designed 2002 model, and he owns one

298
00:24:16,761 --> 00:24:19,240
of the originals, the 57 T-Bird.

299
00:24:19,980 --> 00:24:23,500
That year, Ford presented several convertible designs.

300
00:24:24,160 --> 00:24:28,880
The Thunderbird offered a removable hardtop in addition to the standard soft top.

301
00:24:29,380 --> 00:24:34,520
Its stablemate, the Skyliner, introduced America's first retractable hardtop,

302
00:24:35,080 --> 00:24:38,360
a stowable hardtop that tucked completely into the truck.

303
00:24:39,240 --> 00:24:44,280
The very first retractable hardtop, believe it or not, was not mass-produced in America.

304
00:24:44,520 --> 00:24:46,260
It was mass-produced in France.

305
00:24:46,840 --> 00:24:50,540
It was a Peugeot Eclipse in about the mid-to-late 30s.

306
00:24:50,541 --> 00:24:54,040
The top really wasn't as well engineered as it could have been.

307
00:24:54,041 --> 00:24:58,480
But at the time, they had limited engineering expertise in that area,

308
00:24:58,481 --> 00:25:01,380
and it was pretty brave to try that,

309
00:25:01,381 --> 00:25:04,060
and I give them a lot of credit for putting it out there.

310
00:25:05,280 --> 00:25:09,520
In 1941, Chrysler had experimented with the retractable

311
00:25:09,521 --> 00:25:11,860
in its concept car, the Thunderbolt.

312
00:25:12,460 --> 00:25:16,840
Dubbed the car of the future, its exorbitant price of $19,000

313
00:25:16,841 --> 00:25:19,900
limited its production to just six cars.

314
00:25:20,540 --> 00:25:23,440
Only four of those cars are still known to exist.

315
00:25:24,720 --> 00:25:29,140
Nobody had ever seen anything quite like this newest new kind of Ford,

316
00:25:29,141 --> 00:25:32,980
the Skyliner, the world's only hideaway hardtop.

317
00:25:34,620 --> 00:25:40,600
Unquestionably, the most impressive retractable hardtop was the 1957 Ford Skyliner.

318
00:25:41,400 --> 00:25:47,300
17 and a half feet long, with a 350 pound, 7 foot long hardtop,

319
00:25:47,301 --> 00:25:53,640
and costing $2,942, it took a lot of muscle to bring this car to market.

320
00:25:56,600 --> 00:26:00,160
I guess Ford looked at it as, how can I get the best of both worlds?

321
00:26:00,260 --> 00:26:02,980
How can I have a convertible but yet still have a steel roof?

322
00:26:03,500 --> 00:26:06,100
And I think Ford's idea was pretty interesting.

323
00:26:07,560 --> 00:26:11,140
Originally intended for the 1956 Continental Mark II,

324
00:26:12,040 --> 00:26:14,360
engineers remembered the aborted project,

325
00:26:14,361 --> 00:26:18,680
tried it on their Fairlane 500, and the Skyliner was born.

326
00:26:19,640 --> 00:26:26,000
This car has seven motors that do the lifting and the tucking and the screwing down.

327
00:26:26,720 --> 00:26:30,980
These seven or eight relays in there basically do the positioning and turning on of the motors.

328
00:26:31,580 --> 00:26:34,200
And then there's about 500 to 600 feet of cabling,

329
00:26:34,480 --> 00:26:38,500
which allows all the information to go around the car to turn on the motors

330
00:26:38,501 --> 00:26:40,520
to tell when the motors to stop,

331
00:26:40,521 --> 00:26:44,020
when positioning of the top is located,

332
00:26:44,140 --> 00:26:46,920
where the positioning of the flapper is located, and so forth,

333
00:26:47,080 --> 00:26:51,420
all that gets tied back through one little button back here.

334
00:26:53,600 --> 00:26:56,180
Promoted for its safety as well as convenience,

335
00:26:56,740 --> 00:27:02,120
the Skyliner went on to sell a respectable 20,700 vehicles in its first year.

336
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:06,040
But the retracting mechanism had one major problem.

337
00:27:06,041 --> 00:27:10,440
If any minor part broke down, the operation could stop midway.

338
00:27:10,520 --> 00:27:13,360
making the car nearly impossible to drive.

339
00:27:14,500 --> 00:27:17,240
Over the next two years, sales retreated,

340
00:27:17,460 --> 00:27:22,240
and the 1959 Skyliner was Ford's last hardtop retractable.

341
00:27:23,380 --> 00:27:27,160
To this day, the Skyliner stands out as an engineering masterpiece,

342
00:27:27,680 --> 00:27:30,380
a classic in one of the great eras of a convertible.

343
00:27:30,381 --> 00:27:34,620
The only cars to compare were the flamboyant convertibles of 1959

344
00:27:34,621 --> 00:27:37,740
when design reached unrestrained proportions.

345
00:27:37,741 --> 00:27:43,980
The name Thunderbird comes from the Native American myth of a bird that created thunder

346
00:27:43,981 --> 00:27:47,540
and lightning.

347
00:27:47,541 --> 00:27:52,100
The end of the 1950s marked a turning point for convertible designs.

348
00:27:52,101 --> 00:27:56,400
The sporty two-door T-Bird had grown into a heftier four-seater.

349
00:27:56,401 --> 00:27:59,300
The Skyliner had come and gone.

350
00:27:59,301 --> 00:28:04,500
The trend was again towards big, and designers were pushing the limits of automotive style

351
00:28:04,501 --> 00:28:08,840
with the most outrageous models the history of the convertible had ever seen.

352
00:28:09,900 --> 00:28:13,760
The 59, the fins reached their apex, both literally and figuratively.

353
00:28:14,460 --> 00:28:18,640
There were very few production cars with fins higher than the 1959 Cadillac.

354
00:28:21,420 --> 00:28:25,360
1959 witnessed the climax in the Battle of the Tailfin.

355
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:30,940
The year before, Cadillac had been outfinned by Chrysler with their imperial crown.

356
00:28:30,941 --> 00:28:37,940
The engineers at Cadillac, determined to fight back, offered options like cruise control,

357
00:28:37,941 --> 00:28:41,516
electric door locks, and automatic headlight dimmers.

358
00:28:41,517 --> 00:28:44,080
Designers added two taillights because

359
00:28:44,081 --> 00:28:49,636
two was better than one. And the fins reached spectacular proportions.

360
00:28:49,637 --> 00:28:51,700
For many in the automotive

361
00:28:51,701 --> 00:28:55,900
world, the 59 Cadillac fins exceeded the limits of good taste.

362
00:28:55,901 --> 00:28:58,000
Yet the competition decided not

363
00:28:58,001 --> 00:29:03,040
to forsake the fins, but simply turned them on their side.

364
00:29:03,041 --> 00:29:07,460
This is a 1959 Chevrolet Impala convertible.

365
00:29:07,461 --> 00:29:12,820
Back in 59, they did have optional motors, and you could get a six-cylinder, or you could

366
00:29:12,821 --> 00:29:17,340
get a medium-sized V8 with a two-barrel or four-barrel.

367
00:29:17,341 --> 00:29:21,020
And the Impala came with all the extra, that was the top of the line.

368
00:29:21,021 --> 00:29:23,180
And the convertibles only came in Impalas.

369
00:29:23,181 --> 00:29:26,880
So you got all the chrome and the stainless steel and all the fancy stuff.

370
00:29:28,840 --> 00:29:32,460
1959 was the breakthrough year for Chevrolet's designers.

371
00:29:32,960 --> 00:29:37,820
They had converted the ho-hum 58 Impala and transformed it into a masterpiece.

372
00:29:39,160 --> 00:29:42,240
In the earlier Chevrolet's, they had the fins that were standing on end.

373
00:29:43,020 --> 00:29:47,220
And to get the public's attention again, they laid them down flat, and it worked out very well.

374
00:29:48,040 --> 00:29:52,580
And with a cat-eye type of tail light, the only year they had a cat-eye tail light in it.

375
00:29:53,180 --> 00:29:59,600
They had dual antennas, and then they had rockets on the fenders with vapor trail.

376
00:29:59,920 --> 00:30:00,640
So it really was.

377
00:30:00,680 --> 00:30:05,180
It was a car that really got the attention of the public, the young public, and it did very well in 59.

378
00:30:07,600 --> 00:30:14,560
By the end of the decade, convertibles like the Impala were making their presence known on every highway in America.

379
00:30:15,080 --> 00:30:21,180
Almost 10% of all cars sold were convertibles, up from 2% just 10 years earlier.

380
00:30:21,181 --> 00:30:27,860
The fascination for the convertible would be threatened, however, as the 1960s rolled around.

381
00:30:30,340 --> 00:30:35,860
Today, that era is viewed as a time of nonconformity and excessive self-expression.

382
00:30:36,900 --> 00:30:40,420
But back then, car buyers were looking for just the opposite.

383
00:30:41,000 --> 00:30:45,340
They wanted simplicity, tiring of the overstated designs of the 50s.

384
00:30:45,341 --> 00:30:54,100
In fact, the steadiest selling convertibles in the early 1960s were the rather staid Studebaker Lark and the Rambler American.

385
00:30:55,300 --> 00:30:59,220
Fun is what's new in Falcon, the all-time economy champ.

386
00:30:59,500 --> 00:31:01,000
And now it's convertible, too.

387
00:31:02,160 --> 00:31:09,540
Cars like the Falcon Futura and its younger cousin, the Falcon Sprint, were big sellers for Ford in the early 1960s.

388
00:31:09,541 --> 00:31:15,340
They led the market for a cheap-to-buy, cheap-to-run, middle-of-the-road convertible.

389
00:31:15,341 --> 00:31:21,040
But the sprint lasted just three years as Ford gave the car's chassis a more aggressive

390
00:31:21,041 --> 00:31:22,040
look.

391
00:31:22,041 --> 00:31:27,160
It was the beginning of the pony car, the nickname for the Mustang.

392
00:31:27,161 --> 00:31:33,540
In 1965, Ford introduced the youthful two-door Mustang, and it became wildly popular.

393
00:31:33,541 --> 00:31:38,540
You could dress it up or dress it down, make it expensive, make it inexpensive, make it

394
00:31:38,541 --> 00:31:43,320
sporty, make it utilitarian if you wanted, just depending on the kinds of options that

395
00:31:43,321 --> 00:31:47,700
you ordered. You could get a convertible either power operated or manually operated.

396
00:31:48,500 --> 00:31:55,500
In the first model year alone, nearly 102,000 Mustang convertibles were sold, toppling several

397
00:31:55,501 --> 00:31:58,069
popular models that just couldn't compete.

398
00:31:58,070 --> 00:32:00,700
One of those cars, considered innovative at

399
00:32:00,701 --> 00:32:04,098
the time, was, believe it or not, the Corvair.

400
00:32:04,099 --> 00:32:07,200
The radical design of both its hardtop and

401
00:32:07,201 --> 00:32:13,460
Convertible models, including unibody construction and an air-cooled engine placed in the rear of the car,

402
00:32:13,980 --> 00:32:16,800
did well when it was first introduced in 1960.

403
00:32:17,840 --> 00:32:22,220
But within two years, the Corvair was being condemned as a death trap.

404
00:32:22,960 --> 00:32:26,600
By 1964, a significant number of Corvair drivers,

405
00:32:27,060 --> 00:32:30,540
unprepared for the handling characteristics of its rear engine design,

406
00:32:30,541 --> 00:32:33,960
were involved in serious, often fatal, accidents.

407
00:32:33,961 --> 00:32:40,460
The Corvair's reputation buckled under the pressure of consumer activist Ralph Nader's

408
00:32:40,461 --> 00:32:43,560
campaign to abolish the car.

409
00:32:43,561 --> 00:32:49,720
By 1966, despite dramatic changes to the Corvair's design, any hope of its competing with the

410
00:32:49,721 --> 00:32:51,840
Mustang were gone.

411
00:32:51,841 --> 00:32:57,040
Nader's crusade helped kill off the Corvair, and in the process made automotive safety

412
00:32:57,041 --> 00:32:59,540
a top priority for the consumer.

413
00:32:59,541 --> 00:33:05,960
By the late 1960s, the convertible's appeal was diminishing, overloaded by fears of unprotected

414
00:33:05,961 --> 00:33:06,960
driving.

415
00:33:06,961 --> 00:33:13,220
In 1965, production of convertibles as a proportion to hardtops peaked.

416
00:33:13,221 --> 00:33:19,920
And then after that, it went steadily down, and one manufacturer after another discontinued

417
00:33:19,921 --> 00:33:22,620
the convertible from their line.

418
00:33:22,621 --> 00:33:26,820
Most new car buyers were finding comfort in their sensible sedans and station wagons.

419
00:33:26,821 --> 00:33:36,100
But while car enthusiasts may have forsaken the convertible, they hadn't completely lost

420
00:33:36,101 --> 00:33:37,100
their edge.

421
00:33:37,101 --> 00:33:43,640
In search of new excitement, drivers had replaced the convertible with the muscle car and acceleration

422
00:33:43,641 --> 00:33:48,900
was what it was all about.

423
00:33:48,901 --> 00:33:56,000
We're looking at a very unusual car and quite rare.

424
00:33:56,001 --> 00:34:02,620
A lot of people like Chevelles, Coupes, convertibles, and this is one of 17 only.

425
00:34:03,180 --> 00:34:08,520
Only 17 of this LS6 with a big engine convertibles made.

426
00:34:09,240 --> 00:34:17,000
And this is the largest motor that Chevrolet has ever put in a street car, this big 450, 454.

427
00:34:17,001 --> 00:34:18,700
or so it really goes.

428
00:34:25,380 --> 00:34:29,660
The fact that only 17 of the thousands of big-engine Chevelles

429
00:34:29,661 --> 00:34:31,740
were convertibles tells the story.

430
00:34:32,480 --> 00:34:35,360
Despite their popularity just five years before,

431
00:34:35,800 --> 00:34:38,080
convertibles were fast approaching extinction,

432
00:34:38,860 --> 00:34:42,840
their numbers tumbling to just 1.5% of the American market.

433
00:34:43,260 --> 00:34:44,900
And it would get worse.

434
00:34:47,000 --> 00:34:52,980
After 1973, the fabulous Ford Mustang convertible ceased to exist.

435
00:34:54,340 --> 00:35:01,700
And in 1976, after 30 years, the classic American convertible, the Buick LeSabre, was gone.

436
00:35:02,920 --> 00:35:08,000
That left only one convertible still being produced in America, the Cadillac.

437
00:35:08,760 --> 00:35:11,680
The last Cadillac convertible was an El Dorado.

438
00:35:12,160 --> 00:35:16,120
A very highly evolved car when you think about it, not particularly well packaged,

439
00:35:16,121 --> 00:35:19,820
but it had front-wheel drive, it had four-wheel disc brakes,

440
00:35:19,821 --> 00:35:23,620
it had a power top, it had every possible comfort and convenience,

441
00:35:23,621 --> 00:35:26,620
and optionally you could get fuel injection too.

442
00:35:26,621 --> 00:35:29,620
But this was the mid-'70s,

443
00:35:29,621 --> 00:35:33,120
the time of gas shortages, economic downturns,

444
00:35:33,121 --> 00:35:35,620
and heightened concerns for safety.

445
00:35:35,621 --> 00:35:38,620
Not the time for the monstrous Eldorado.

446
00:35:38,621 --> 00:35:42,620
The 76 Cadillac convertible is huge.

447
00:35:42,621 --> 00:35:44,120
You sit there and you look out,

448
00:35:44,121 --> 00:35:46,120
and you've got an acre of sheet metal in front of you,

449
00:35:46,121 --> 00:35:47,120
or at least it looks like that,

450
00:35:47,121 --> 00:35:49,120
and you really have to plan your turns in advance.

451
00:35:49,121 --> 00:35:52,120
It's not the kind of car you want to throw into a corner.

452
00:35:52,121 --> 00:35:56,120
General Motors watched their sales fall dramatically,

453
00:35:56,121 --> 00:36:00,120
and on April 21, 1976, the last American convertible

454
00:36:00,121 --> 00:36:02,120
rolled off the assembly line.

455
00:36:02,121 --> 00:36:04,120
It was the end of an era.

456
00:36:04,121 --> 00:36:08,120
Since 1915, there had always been an American convertible.

457
00:36:08,121 --> 00:36:12,120
It would be years before its resurrection.

458
00:36:12,121 --> 00:36:20,960
The name for General Motors' flagship sports car, the Corvette, was the brainchild of Myron Scott, who also started the All-American Soapbox Derby.

459
00:36:22,660 --> 00:36:31,500
Though the 1970s marked the end of the American convertible, the European car makers had continued their love affair with open-top driving.

460
00:36:32,180 --> 00:36:36,020
The British had their MGs and Jaguar XKEs.

461
00:36:36,660 --> 00:36:42,080
And the supercars from Italy continued their tradition of the sleek, powerful sports car.

462
00:36:42,120 --> 00:36:47,400
In 1967, Ferrari came out with the 365 Spyder.

463
00:36:47,401 --> 00:36:54,360
In hopes of attracting the American market, they dubbed it the California.

464
00:36:54,361 --> 00:37:01,240
The Europeans had also addressed the public's clamoring for safety with the innovative Targa top.

465
00:37:01,241 --> 00:37:06,280
Back in 1965, Porsche introduced their 911 convertible.

466
00:37:06,281 --> 00:37:11,480
The car featured a lift-off hardtop and a folding plastic rear window.

467
00:37:11,481 --> 00:37:19,760
In between stood a safety hoop, a fixed roll bar, which added protection for the passengers and increased rigidity to the car.

468
00:37:20,520 --> 00:37:25,900
It was the perfect solution for drivers who wanted both safety and open-top driving.

469
00:37:26,760 --> 00:37:37,941
By the early 1980s, Detroit noticed that convertible enthusiasts with no other choice were buying European designs and older American convertibles in increasing

470
00:37:37,942 --> 00:37:38,642
numbers.

471
00:37:38,643 --> 00:37:41,500
They decided to do something about it.

472
00:37:41,501 --> 00:37:46,660
In Detroit, Chrysler in particular looked and said, hmm, maybe that wasn't such a good

473
00:37:46,661 --> 00:37:49,740
idea, eliminating the convertible from the model line.

474
00:37:49,741 --> 00:37:55,740
So in 1982, Chrysler brought back a model of the K car as a convertible.

475
00:37:55,741 --> 00:38:00,400
Around that time, Lee Iacocca was desperately trying to save Chrysler from the hands of

476
00:38:00,401 --> 00:38:06,180
its creditors and looking for any marketing angle he could find.

477
00:38:06,181 --> 00:38:12,540
In a moment of caprice, Iacocca had his engineers lop off the top of his newly designed K-Car

478
00:38:12,541 --> 00:38:18,620
LeBaron, and he personally drove it for the summer.

479
00:38:18,621 --> 00:38:24,180
The reaction to the convertible was overwhelming, and Chrysler immediately went into production.

480
00:38:24,181 --> 00:38:32,860
They sold 25,000 LeBaron convertibles in 1982, Chrysler's first convertible in 11 years.

481
00:38:32,861 --> 00:38:38,200
Surprised by the LeBaron convertible's success, Ford and GM quickly returned to the convertible

482
00:38:38,201 --> 00:38:41,200
market.

483
00:38:41,201 --> 00:38:46,020
Ford brought back its Mustang and GM the Corvette convertible.

484
00:38:46,021 --> 00:38:51,220
Enthusiasm for the convertible was growing slowly until a small, inexpensive import from

485
00:38:51,221 --> 00:38:55,220
Japan hit the American market.

486
00:38:55,221 --> 00:39:02,380
The Mazda MX-5 Miata, a tiny four-cylinder two-seater, was brought to America in 1989

487
00:39:02,381 --> 00:39:05,440
and created an immediate sensation.

488
00:39:05,441 --> 00:39:08,920
Priced at just $16,000, in many cases,

489
00:39:08,921 --> 00:39:12,160
the cars were sold for twice that amount.

490
00:39:12,161 --> 00:39:16,120
The Miata had ignited the public's imagination.

491
00:39:16,121 --> 00:39:17,620
You sit back.

492
00:39:17,621 --> 00:39:20,560
You just kind of wind the gears out a little bit more,

493
00:39:20,561 --> 00:39:24,220
get the tack, maybe $5,000 or $6,000.

494
00:39:24,221 --> 00:39:26,500
And maybe you're not the fastest car on the road,

495
00:39:26,501 --> 00:39:28,100
but you feel like it.

496
00:39:28,101 --> 00:39:29,820
And it's not the same if you have a hard top.

497
00:39:29,821 --> 00:39:30,521
It just isn't.

498
00:39:32,380 --> 00:39:38,880
Borrowing liberally from the designs of the two-seat European sports car, Mazda created

499
00:39:38,881 --> 00:39:44,140
the Miata specifically to tug at the heart of the American convertible market, Southern

500
00:39:44,141 --> 00:39:45,140
California.

501
00:39:45,141 --> 00:39:50,380
There's a bit of a cache to it, and I'm positive that whenever I'm driving down the

502
00:39:50,381 --> 00:39:55,560
road or when anybody's driving down the road and the top is down on their car, men and

503
00:39:55,561 --> 00:40:01,560
women, they're going to pull up a little bit to see, ooh, you know, is he hot, is she hot?

504
00:40:01,561 --> 00:40:05,100
So it automatically gets the attention.

505
00:40:05,101 --> 00:40:10,560
The Miata's enormous impact on California driving soon spread to the rest of the nation,

506
00:40:10,561 --> 00:40:16,460
and the convertible's image as the epitome of driving excitement had been resurrected.

507
00:40:16,461 --> 00:40:22,400
The revived enthusiasm for the smaller convertible was filling coffers of the car companies and

508
00:40:22,401 --> 00:40:26,340
opening new opportunities for their engineers.

509
00:40:26,341 --> 00:40:31,580
Car designers recognized that the smaller cars meant shorter and lighter tops and jumped

510
00:40:31,581 --> 00:40:37,460
at the chance to bring back an old friend, the retractable hardtop.

511
00:40:37,461 --> 00:40:42,480
They hoped to conquer the problems of the massive Skyliner and finally bring together

512
00:40:42,481 --> 00:40:48,660
the safety and comfort of a hardtop and the freedom of open-top driving.

513
00:40:48,661 --> 00:40:55,960
Mitsubishi tried their hand at the retractable hardtop in 1997 with the Spyder 3000.

514
00:40:55,961 --> 00:41:03,540
But the additional cost of $20,000 priced the $62,000 car right out of the market.

515
00:41:03,541 --> 00:41:11,760
In 1998, Mercedes-Benz broke through with their retractable hardtop, the SLK.

516
00:41:11,761 --> 00:41:17,720
Three years later, Lexus introduced its own retractable, the SC430.

517
00:41:17,721 --> 00:41:21,640
With its top up, it's sleek, safe, and quiet.

518
00:41:21,641 --> 00:41:25,500
23 seconds later, you have a sporty open-top convertible.

519
00:41:25,820 --> 00:41:30,720
You literally use your index finger, push it down, hold it down for 23 seconds,

520
00:41:30,820 --> 00:41:31,580
and it's like magic.

521
00:41:31,680 --> 00:41:35,280
The whole thing opens up and it goes into the trunk, and suddenly you're a convertible.

522
00:41:35,840 --> 00:41:41,160
Seven separate motors open and close the trunk, center panel, and side windows.

523
00:41:41,960 --> 00:41:44,820
Three more lift the aluminum top on and off.

524
00:41:45,760 --> 00:41:49,640
Every phase of the process is monitored by 12 electronic switches,

525
00:41:49,641 --> 00:41:52,820
and it is all controlled by a central computer.

526
00:41:53,740 --> 00:41:58,600
Wall off, 23 seconds, and you've got a whole new reality.

527
00:41:59,520 --> 00:42:02,660
A new reality, the best of both worlds.

528
00:42:03,280 --> 00:42:05,680
That's the experience of the newest convertibles.

529
00:42:08,020 --> 00:42:12,720
In the future, it's certain that more and more hardtop retractables will come to market,

530
00:42:13,280 --> 00:42:15,740
and the ragtop may bid us farewell.

531
00:42:15,741 --> 00:42:19,740
But for every convertible owner, it's never been about the top.

532
00:42:19,840 --> 00:42:22,740
It's been about the freedom to choose.

